# ⚠ THIS FILE DOES NOTHING ON THE CURRENT HOST.
#
# GitHub Pages issues no redirects from a rules file. What it DOES do, without this file, is the one
# redirect this site actually needs: with `doctoracuevillas.com` in CNAME, requests to
# `www.doctoracuevillas.com` are redirected to the apex, provided the `www` DNS record points at the
# Pages host. That is the canonical-identity rule satisfied by the host rather than by this file.
#
# Everything else here is kept as the specification for a future host, and as the record of what is
# NOT being served today.

# Redirects — Handbook §26.
#
# EVERY URL the predecessor site had that this one does not. The canonical-identity rule is not
# satisfied by choosing a domain: the old URLs are indexed, linked and bookmarked, and they are the
# traffic you already paid for.
#
# A host that cannot issue a real redirect status cannot do this at all. A markup page that refreshes
# the browser is not a redirect — it does not carry the same authority signal and does not work for
# anything that is not markup. That is why hosting is chosen BEFORE the first line of markup (§26).
#
# Syntax is Cloudflare Pages'. 301 is permanent; use 302 only while genuinely temporary.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ canonical host
# The apex is canonical. `www` exists only to be redirected away from, so that one page is never
# reachable at two addresses. On GitHub Pages this is done by the host, from the CNAME file.
https://www.doctoracuevillas.com/*  https://doctoracuevillas.com/:splat  301

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ what CANNOT be redirected here
#
# The predecessor is a Google Sites page at sites.google.com/view/naprofertility. It is on a domain
# this project does not control, so NO RULE IN THIS FILE CAN REACH IT — a redirect is served by the
# host of the OLD address, and that host is Google's. The equity on those URLs is recovered by
# editing the old page itself: leave one line pointing here and remove the rest of the content, so a
# visitor who lands on it moves and a crawler sees the new address. That is a task with an owner in
# brief.md, not a line in this file.
#
# The retired scheduling page naprofertilitydracuevillas.youcanbook.me is in the same position, and
# worse: it is still indexed and still shows old prices. It is de-indexed at the provider. NEVER LINK
# IT FROM HERE — a link would re-establish exactly the signal being removed.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ page URLs
# Not listed, deliberately, and not needed on any host: every page is a directory index, so
# `/fertilidad/` resolves to `fertilidad/index.html` by the oldest rule static servers have. Nothing
# here depends on a host mapping `/fertilidad` to `fertilidad.html`.
